## 1. What the harness actually asserts

The suite is a single Go test file, `tests/suite/calc_test.go` (read-only for this repair), which imports the production package `fixture/buildcodefix/services/calc`:

- `TestAVersion` — fails unless `calc.Version()` returns `2`.
- `TestBCompatibility` — fails when `calc.Version() != 1 && !calc.Compatible()`. In other words it encodes an invariant: *any version other than 1 must also report compatibility support.*

Production source today is a single file, `services/calc/calc.go`:

```
func Version() int     { return 1 }
func Compatible() bool { return false }
```

`TestAVersion` fails (`Version() = 1, want 2`). `TestBCompatibility` currently passes only **vacuously** — the `Version() != 1` conjunct is false, so the `!Compatible()` conjunct is never reached and `Compatible() == false` goes unpunished.

## 2. The trap — this is a cross-walk, not a one-line bump

The moment `Version()` returns `2`, the left conjunct of `TestBCompatibility` becomes true and that test starts actually exercising `Compatible()`. A repair that changes only `Version()` turns one red test into a different red test (`version 2 requires compatibility support`) and leaves the suite failing. **Both behaviours must move in the same change.** Do not stop after the version bump; run the whole suite, not just the named failing test.

