**Part A — `Version()` reports 2.**
Change the body of `Version()` so it returns `2`. Keep the exported signature `func Version() int` unchanged; the test calls it with no arguments and compares against an `int`.
*Serves:* `TestAVersion` (the named failing test) — turns `Version() = 1, want 2` green.

**Part B — `Compatible()` reports true.**
Change the body of `Compatible()` so it returns `true`, upholding the invariant that a non-1 version ships compatibility support. Keep the exported signature `func Compatible() bool` unchanged.
*Serves:* `TestBCompatibility`, which Part A promotes from vacuously-passing to genuinely-evaluated. Without Part B the suite stays red.

Two acceptable shapes for Part B — writer's choice, both satisfy the invariant:

- Plain constant: `func Compatible() bool { return true }` — simplest, matches the style of the existing one-line file. This is the recommended default.
- Derived from the version, e.g. returning whether `Version()` is at least 2 — makes the invariant self-documenting in the source. Only pick this if you keep it exactly equivalent to `true` at the current version; it must not reintroduce `false` for `Version() == 2`.

Optionally add a short comment above `Compatible()` recording *why* it is true (the version-2 compatibility invariant the suite enforces), so a future version bump does not silently re-break the pair. Comments only — no behavioural extras.

